  "content": {
    "title": "El Niño watch issued by NOAA for summer. Here's what it means for CT",
    "text": "On Thursday, NOAA's Climate Prediction Center issued an El Niño watch, signaling that the large-scale climate pattern could develop within the next six months. For now, a La Niña advisory remains in effect as sea surface temperatures in the equatorial Pacific Ocean continue to show signs of gradually returning to neutral. Forecasters expect that transition to continue through the spring before conditions potentially shift into El Niño territory later this summer. By autumn, the CPC says there's a 1-in-3 chance that a \"strong\" El Niño could form, sometimes referred to as a \"super\" El Niño. Why the change in outlook? The CPC says several signals in the Pacific Ocean are pointing in that direction. In its latest discussion, the agency noted that “the increasing odds of El Niño are supported by the large amount of heat in the subsurface ocean and the expected weakening of the low-level trade winds.” El Niño is part of a larger climate cycle known as the El Niño–Southern Oscillation, or ENSO for short. During an El Niño phase, sea surface temperatures across the central and eastern tropical Pacific run warmer than average. That added warmth can shift atmospheric circulation patterns across the tropics and ripple outward, subtly influencing weather patterns around the globe. What could El Niño mean for Connecticut? The answer isn’t always straightforward. ENSO is just one of several large-scale climate patterns that influence weather across the Northeast, and its effects are usually measured over months rather than days. In other words, El Niño doesn’t determine the forecast for any single afternoon, but it can gently nudge seasonal trends in one direction or another. If El Niño does develop by late summer, it could influence weather patterns heading into autumn. Historically, El Niño years tend to favor a more active southern storm track across the United States later in the year. For the Northeast, that sometimes translates to slightly wetter-than-average conditions during the fall months as more storm systems move through the region. Temperature trends can vary, but autumns during El Niño years in the Northeast often lean milder overall. That’s because the jet stream tends to track a bit farther south, allowing warmer air to linger across parts of the eastern United States for longer stretches. Of course, those are broad seasonal tendencies rather than guarantees. Even in strong El Niño years, day-to-day weather in Connecticut will still be driven by the usual parade of cold fronts, high-pressure systems and coastal storms that define our region’s climate. What the El Niño watch does signal, however, is that scientists are seeing early ingredients in place for a potential shift in the Pacific, one that could quietly shape weather patterns across much of the globe in the months ahead.",
